On a complete lattice $L$, write $e : L \to L$ for an endomap. We say $e$ is **idempotent** when $e \circ e = e$ (equivalently $e(e x) = e x$ for all $x$). An element $x \in L$ is a **fixed point** of $e$ when $e(x) = x$.

Scott’s discussion after Theorem 2.12 treats a retraction $j : \mathcal{P}I \to \mathcal{P}I$ as idempotent and identifies its range with $\{ X : j(X) = X \}$. The proof of Theorem 2.12 uses the same fixed-point subtype for the idempotent $e = s \circ r$ built from an injective-space retraction into a Sierpiński power.

**Definition (`IdempotentFixedPoint`).** For $e : L \to L$ on a complete lattice $L$, **`IdempotentFixedPoint e`** is the subtype $\{ x \in L \mid e x = x \}$.

